{"type":"message","id":"a9b46cd0","parentId":"dd0518f2","timestamp":"2026-05-12T17:38:34.977Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[media attached: /home/openclaw/.openclaw/media/inbound/unstoppable_fabric_deep_dive_into_scalable_ai_ready_spark_in---7100aa1a-1ffd-421b-9653-7568e557dae1.pdf (application/pdf) | /home/openclaw/.openclaw/media/inbound/unstoppable_fabric_deep_dive_into_scalable_ai_ready_spark_in---7100aa1a-1ffd-421b-9653-7568e557dae1.pdf]\n[Telegram OpenClaw Bunker id:8785888003 Tue 2026-05-12 19:38 GMT+2] <media:document>\n\n<file name=\"unstoppable_fabric_deep_dive_into_scalable_ai_ready_spark_in---7100aa1a-1ffd-421b-9653-7568e557dae1.pdf\" mime=\"application/pdf\">\n\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d248728b0d1b6c4a\">>>\nSource: External\n---\nUnstoppable Fabric: Deep Dive into Scalable, AI-Ready Spark in Microsoft Fabric\n\nUnstoppable Fabric: Deep Dive into Scalable, AI-Ready Spark in Microsoft Fabric Iurii (Yurri) Iurchenko Sr Data Engineer, Azure Advocate, Utah Fabric Group Co- lead\n\nAgenda In this session, we’ll take a practical deep dive into Spark -based data engineering in Microsoft Fabric. We’ll walk through proven techniques and repeatable patterns for solving common and advanced data engineering challenges, covering advanced Spark SQL functions, effective approaches to semi-structured data, and strategies for building reliable, maintainable pipelines. We will be focusing on Fabric-specific aspects of Spark Notebooks, including table optimization. AI-related capabilities in Fabric Spark will also be covered, including built-in and integrated functions that support text processing, enrichment, classification, and feature engineering directly within Spark workloads, along with guidance on where these functions fit. Audience: The presentation is technical and aimed at data engineers and analytics engineers using Spark in Fabric. The session is beginner-friendly, with roughly 50% focused on advanced techniques.\n\nAgenda 01 Contexts for Spark Usage Healthcare Data, API data, RealTime Data & NRT, Operational Automation, Complex Logic, Data Cleaning 02 What is Spark’s Mission in Fabric? Medallion, PySpark, Spark SQL, Python Notebooks, One Lake, Alternative Power Query, Data Factory, & AI 03 Technical Level in Spark Idempotence (overwriting, partitioning, merging), API (explode, text, jsonl), Custom libraries (UDF) + its performance, Multithreading read, Delta tables optimization, dedup & cleaning, performance 04 AI & Spark – From Good to Great Fundamentals (fuzziness, categories, conclusion, additional info), Custom AI functions, Spark & ML 05 Fabric’s Spark Modularity (two versions), Environments, Key Vault, Capacity Consumption & high concurrency 06 Spark – Decision Making & Conclusions Open floor and action items\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d248728b0d1b6c4a\">>>\n</file>"}],"timestamp":1778607514971}}
{"type":"custom_message","customType":"openclaw.runtime-context","content":"[media attached: /home/openclaw/.openclaw/media/inbound/unstoppable_fabric_deep_dive_into_scalable_ai_ready_spark_in---7100aa1a-1ffd-421b-9653-7568e557dae1.pdf (application/pdf) | /home/openclaw/.openclaw/media/inbound/unstoppable_fabric_deep_dive_into_scalable_ai_ready_spark_in---7100aa1a-1ffd-421b-9653-7568e557dae1.pdf]\nTo send an image back, prefer the message tool (media/path/filePath). If you must inline, use MEDIA:https://example.com/image.jpg (spaces ok, quote if needed) or a safe relative path like MEDIA:./image.jpg. Absolute and ~ paths only work when they stay inside your allowed file-read boundary; host file:// URLs are blocked. Keep caption in the text body.\nConversation info (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"chat_id\": \"telegram:8785888003\",\n  \"message_id\": \"1210\",\n  \"sender_id\": \"8785888003\",\n  \"sender\": \"OpenClaw Bunker\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"Tue 2026-05-12 19:38 GMT+2\"\n}\n```\n\nSender (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"OpenClaw Bunker (8785888003)\",\n  \"id\": \"8785888003\",\n  \"name\": \"OpenClaw Bunker\"\n}\n```\n\n[Telegram OpenClaw Bunker id:8785888003 Tue 2026-05-12 19:38 GMT+2] <media:document>\n\n<file name=\"unstoppable_fabric_deep_dive_into_scalable_ai_ready_spark_in---7100aa1a-1ffd-421b-9653-7568e557dae1.pdf\" mime=\"application/pdf\">\n\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d248728b0d1b6c4a\">>>\nSource: External\n---\nUnstoppable Fabric: Deep Dive into Scalable, AI-Ready Spark in Microsoft Fabric\n\nUnstoppable Fabric: Deep Dive into Scalable, AI-Ready Spark in Microsoft Fabric Iurii (Yurri) Iurchenko Sr Data Engineer, Azure Advocate, Utah Fabric Group Co- lead\n\nAgenda In this session, we’ll take a practical deep dive into Spark -based data engineering in Microsoft Fabric. We’ll walk through proven techniques and repeatable patterns for solving common and advanced data engineering challenges, covering advanced Spark SQL functions, effective approaches to semi-structured data, and strategies for building reliable, maintainable pipelines. We will be focusing on Fabric-specific aspects of Spark Notebooks, including table optimization. AI-related capabilities in Fabric Spark will also be covered, including built-in and integrated functions that support text processing, enrichment, classification, and feature engineering directly within Spark workloads, along with guidance on where these functions fit. Audience: The presentation is technical and aimed at data engineers and analytics engineers using Spark in Fabric. The session is beginner-friendly, with roughly 50% focused on advanced techniques.\n\nAgenda 01 Contexts for Spark Usage Healthcare Data, API data, RealTime Data & NRT, Operational Automation, Complex Logic, Data Cleaning 02 What is Spark’s Mission in Fabric? Medallion, PySpark, Spark SQL, Python Notebooks, One Lake, Alternative Power Query, Data Factory, & AI 03 Technical Level in Spark Idempotence (overwriting, partitioning, merging), API (explode, text, jsonl), Custom libraries (UDF) + its performance, Multithreading read, Delta tables optimization, dedup & cleaning, performance 04 AI & Spark – From Good to Great Fundamentals (fuzziness, categories, conclusion, additional info), Custom AI functions, Spark & ML 05 Fabric’s Spark Modularity (two versions), Environments, Key Vault, Capacity Consumption & high concurrency 06 Spark – Decision Making & Conclusions Open floor and action items\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d248728b0d1b6c4a\">>>\n</file>","display":false,"details":{"source":"openclaw-runtime-context"},"id":"517e0b92","parentId":"a9b46cd0","timestamp":"2026-05-12T17:38:34.982Z"}
